{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1794600405,"nonce":"0KBlIG6JEgnLE+A16oa0vC17658=","target":"6ca91683d4c29e8c7dcc7283d9446456d96c1634717e5cb00d436fe8568aa6ff","message":"The main thread blocks 0.1 to 1 s in the drawable acquire at focus and occlusion changes on macOS\n\nThe frame path acquires and releases its CAMetalDrawable on the main thread, which is IPC to the window server, and a stall there delays every event. Seen in flip_window_check as gaps of 114 to 967 ms: 8 in 12 minutes of 30 s runs, and none of them a slow phase of the frame. Stack samples taken as a stall began put the main thread in -[CAMetalLayer nextDrawable] (its usleep poll) and in CAMetalDrawable create and release, within 160 ms of a Focused(false) or Occluded(true). The timer thread was on time throughout.\n\nDropping frames for a covered window (f9170ae) removes the work while covered. It does not remove the stall at the transition itself, where the frame in flight can still block, nor a stall caused by something else loading the window server. The longer ones (0.3 to 1 s) never reproduced on demand, only ~110 ms ones at an occlusion change.\n\nDiagnosis aid: a watchdog thread on the loop's heartbeat (new_events and about_to_wait) that logs the loop state and runs `sample \u003cpid\u003e 1 1` after 100 ms of silence names the hop in one run. Not kept in the tree.\n\nOpen design question: acquire the drawable off the main thread, or bound the wait (a shorter drawable timeout, dropping the frame when it expires).\n\nRelated: f87e115 (closed), 77fc97d.","files":null}]}